---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { person, website, SITE } from '../lib/seo';

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'ProfilePage',
    '@id': new URL('/about/', SITE).href,
    url: new URL('/about/', SITE).href,
    name: 'About Rayhana bint Estafanos al-Abbas',
    mainEntity: { '@id': person['@id'] },
    isPartOf: { '@id': website['@id'] },
  },
  {
    ...person,
    description:
      'SCA member since 2005 with a 16th-century Ottoman persona; Kingdom Chatelaine of Caid. Researches and teaches Ottoman dress, food, and performance.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="About me"
  description="Rayhana bint Estafanos al-Abbas: SCA member since 2005, 16th-century Ottoman persona, Kingdom Chatelaine of Caid. Classes taught, competitions, and how to get in touch."
  image="/images/rayhana-portrait.jpg"
  imageAlt="Rayhana in a block-printed caftan and red tarpuş"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="entry-header">
      <p class="rubric">About me</p>
      <h1>Rayhana bint Estafanos al-Abbas</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="about">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="dropcap">
          I joined the SCA in 2005, when I was 16 and looking for better garb for
          the Renaissance faire. I found the garb. I also found the place I&rsquo;ve
          called home ever since.
        </p>
        <p>
          When I met my husband, Suleiman ibn Rawh, I didn&rsquo;t know if it
          would work until he came to an event with me. It worked. These days the
          SCA is a family thing, and our family&rsquo;s photo is on the home page of
          <a href="https://www.sca.org/">SCA.org</a>. We camp with Mama
          Sadb&rsquo;s Caravan of Wonders, and I live in the Barony of Dreiburgen.
        </p>

        <h2>How I ended up Ottoman</h2>
        <p>
          Bellydance. I wanted costumes that were more accurate than what I could
          buy, so I started reading about what women in 16th-century Istanbul wore
          and making it myself. I stopped dancing a long time ago. I kept the
          garb, and I never stopped researching it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That&rsquo;s most of what&rsquo;s on this site: caftans, hats, face
          veils, embroidery, block printing, cooking, Karagöz shadow puppets, and
          one Ottoman gazel I performed at Pentathlon. Each project has its
          documentation, and the class handouts are here to print.
        </p>

        <h2>Rayhana, the persona</h2>
        <p>
          Rayhana lives in the Greek quarter of 16th-century Istanbul with her
          husband Suleiman, an artist known for his &ldquo;magical
          portraits,&rdquo; and their son Hammad, who is learning the trade from
          his parents when he isn&rsquo;t out playing in the streets with the
          other children. She embroiders, paints, cooks, and makes things for her
          family and neighbors, which gives me an excuse to do all of that too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Her arms: <span class="blazon">per fess vert and argent, two domestic
          sea-cats respectant counterchanged.</span>
        </p>

        <h2>Outside the SCA</h2>
        <p>
          My day job is running customer success and support operations at a
          software company: finding where people get stuck and getting it fixed.
          It turns out to be good training for being a chatelaine. It&rsquo;s also
          where I picked up building small tools, which is how the
          <a href="/tools/">pattern generators and calculators</a> on this site
          came about.
        </p>

        <h2>Service</h2>
        <p>
          I&rsquo;ve been Kingdom Chatelaine of Caid since 2024, so most of my
          service time goes to newcomers. I developed the HAIL method of
          chatelaine training, run the newcomers meet-up and the &ldquo;My 1st
          War!&rdquo; ribbons at Great Western War, and helped run the
          WorldCon/LaCon demo in 2026.
        </p>
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>Kingdom Chatelaine, Caid <span class="when">(2024 to present)</span></li>
          <li>Chatelaine, Barony of Dreiburgen <span class="when">(2022 to 2024)</span>: a four-part newcomers series on Zoom, weekly intro classes after the Koroneburg demo, and a restocked loaner garb closet</li>
          <li>Steward, Festival of the Rose Winter <span class="when">(2022)</span>, which moved sites three times during a Covid surge, and Dreiburgen Summer Arts <span class="when">(2023)</span></li>
          <li>Lists Officer, Barony of Gyldenholt <span class="when">(2015 to 2017)</span></li>
          <li>Webwright, Barony of Gyldenholt <span class="when">(2014 to 2016)</span></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Competitions</h2>
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>Gyldenholt Anniversary <span class="when">(2026)</span>: Baronial A&amp;S Champion and People&rsquo;s Choice</li>
          <li>Angels Anniversary <span class="when">(2025)</span>: Hester Spoon Period Cooking Champion</li>
          <li>Dreiburgen Summer Arts <span class="when">(2025)</span>: Baronial A&amp;S Champion, and People&rsquo;s Choice for the <a href="/ans/karagoz-hacivat-puppets/">Karagöz puppets</a></li>
          <li>Lyondemere Anniversary <span class="when">(2024)</span>: Baronial A&amp;S Champion for Minoan garb</li>
          <li>Pentathlon <span class="when">(2022)</span>: 1st place Journeyman in Dramatic Presentation for the <a href="/ans/ottoman-gazel/">gazel</a>, 2nd place Journeyman in Culinary Sweets, 1st place Apprentice in Clothing Accessories</li>
          <li>Dreiburgen Anniversary <span class="when">(2022)</span>: Tortoise winner in the Tortoise and Hare competition, for fabric stamping on the coronation garb of Adam II and Stæina II</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Classes I&rsquo;ve taught</h2>
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li><a href="/ans/ottoman-costuming-basics/">Ottoman Garb Basics</a> <span class="when">(Lyondemere Anniversary 2022, Great Western War 2025, Collegium 2026, PPF Ottoman 2026 on Zoom)</span></li>
          <li><a href="/ans/turkish-pants-salvar/">Making Middle Eastern Pants</a> <span class="when">(Collegium Caidis 2009, Great Western War 2011 and 2021)</span></li>
          <li>Closures: Finish Garb Medievally <span class="when">(Great Western War 2014, Collegium Caidis 2015 and 2018)</span></li>
          <li>Turkish Costume from the Late 15th to 16th Centuries <span class="when">(Collegium Caidis 2012)</span></li>
          <li>Sorguç Make-and-Take and Ottoman Headwear <span class="when">(Crescent Artisan 2026)</span></li>
          <li>Thread Covered Buttons <span class="when">(Collegium 2026)</span></li>
          <li>Henna in Ottoman Turkey <span class="when">(Great Western War 2024)</span></li>
          <li>Near East and Middle Eastern Persona Brunch <span class="when">(Great Western War 2023 and 2024)</span></li>
          <li>Selling the SCA <span class="when">(Great Western War 2025)</span></li>
          <li>Minoan Garb Basics <span class="when">(Dreiburgen Summer Arts 2024)</span></li>
          <li>Small Banner Painting <span class="when">(Basteltag 2023)</span></li>
          <li>Sewing Drop-in, as assistant <span class="when">(Collegium Caidis 2016)</span></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about a project, a handout, or a tool, or want me to teach
          something? Email me at
          <a href="mailto:rayhana@rayhanasrepositorium.com">rayhana@rayhanasrepositorium.com</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="framed">
        <img
          src="/images/rayhana-portrait.jpg"
          width="900"
          height="1350"
          alt="Rayhana seated in front of a fan of peacock feathers, wearing a red tarpuş, a white veil, and a white block-printed caftan with a green sash."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          In my <a href="/ans/the-ottoman-caftan/">caftan</a> and
          <a href="/ans/ottoman-tarpus/">tarpuş</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
